<div class="article-content prose" style="margin-top: 2.5rem; padding-top: 2rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--line);">
  <h2>Step-by-Step Calculation Formula &amp; Worked Example</h2>
  <p>Under the official 4:3 ratio, each Capital One mile yields 0.75 EVA Air miles:</p>
  
  <div class="formula-box" style="margin: 1.5rem 0; padding: 1.25rem; background: var(--ink-2); border-left: 4px solid var(--gold); border-radius: 6px;">
    <div><strong>Transferable Miles</strong> = Floor(Total Miles ÷ 1,000) × 1,000 (minimum 1,000)</div>
    <div style="margin-top: 0.5rem;"><strong>EVA Air Miles Received</strong> = (Transferable Miles ÷ 1,000) × 750 × (1 + Promotional Bonus %)</div>
    <div style="margin-top: 0.5rem;"><strong>Miles Remaining in Capital One</strong> = Total Miles - Transferable Miles</div>
  </div>

  <h3>Worked Real-World Example</h3>
  <p>Suppose you hold <strong>100,500 Capital One miles</strong> and want to book an EVA Air Transpacific flight:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Eligible Transferable Block</strong>: 100,000 miles (500 miles remain in Capital One).</li>
    <li><strong>Base 4:3 Calculation</strong>: 100,000 × 0.75 = <strong>75,000 EVA Air miles</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Bonus Miles</strong>: 0.</li>
    <li><strong>Total Miles Received</strong>: <strong>75,000 Infinity MileageLands miles</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Estimated Travel Value</strong>: At our baseline valuation of 1.4¢ per EVA Air mile, 75,000 miles yield roughly <strong>$1,050</strong> in travel value.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Transfer Rules, Fees, and Sweet Spots</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Why Transfer at 4:3? The Royal Laurel Advantage</strong>: While 4:3 is less than 1:1, EVA Air reserves vastly more transpacific business class award inventory for its own Infinity MileageLands members than it releases to Star Alliance partners (like United or Aeroplan). If you want guaranteed flat-bed seats between North America (SFO, LAX, SEA, JFK, ORD, YVR, YYZ) and Taipei/Asia, EVA's own program is often the only way to book.</li>
    <li><strong>Transfer Fees</strong>: <strong>$0 Free</strong>. Capital One charges no fees to transfer to any partner.</li>
    <li><strong>Transfer Speed</strong>: Transfers are <strong>not instant</strong>. They typically take <strong>24 to 48 business hours</strong> to process. Keep this in mind when timing award hold policies or booking last-minute seats.</li>
    <li><strong>Strictly Non-Reversible</strong>: Transferred miles cannot be returned to Capital One. Confirm inventory before moving points.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Valuation Trade-Off: Is 4:3 Transfer Worth It?</h2>
  <p>Capital One miles carry a versatile baseline valuation of <strong>1.5¢ per point</strong>, with a guaranteed 1.0¢ cash-equivalent floor when using the Purchase Eraser. Factoring in the 4:3 (0.75) ratio, the breakeven benchmark is:</p>
  <p style="text-align: center; font-weight: 500; color: var(--gold);">1.5¢ ÷ 0.75 = 2.0¢ per EVA Air Mile</p>
  <p>Because transpacific round-trip business class tickets on EVA Air frequently retail for $4,000 to $6,000+, redeeming 150,000 to 175,000 miles for such itineraries regularly yields <strong>2.5¢–4.0¢+ per mile</strong>, easily justifying the 4:3 ratio.</p>

